From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: virtudude <virtudude@protonmail.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 0/4] ASoC: qcom: q6apm: robust APM commands

Matches APM responses to their command, adds out-of-band SET_CFG for
large payloads and releases its buffers once the DSP lets go of them,
and tears down graphs left in an unknown state, setting them up again
after a failed start.

 1/4 ASoC: qcom: q6apm: match APM responses to their command
     Author: virtudude <virtudude@protonmail.com>
     Not posted.
 2/4 ASoC: qcom: q6apm: add out-of-band APM SET_CFG
     Author: virtudude <virtudude@protonmail.com>
     Not posted.
 3/4 ASoC: qcom: q6apm: release out-of-band SET_CFG buffers the DSP let go
     Author: virtudude <virtudude@protonmail.com>
     Not posted.
 4/4 ASoC: qcom: q6apm: tear down a graph whose PREPARE, START or STOP failed
     Author: virtudude <virtudude@protonmail.com>
     Not posted.

From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: virtudude <virtudude@protonmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 15:02:19 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] ASoC: qcom: q6apm: match APM responses to their command

q6apm_send_cmd_sync() completes on any response with the expected
opcode and reads one shared apm->result, so a late answer to a command
that timed out can complete the next one: an old UNMAP ack can let a
buffer be freed while the DSP still maps it, and a late GRAPH_CLOSE ack
can complete another graph's close.

Track the command in flight (token, opcode, response opcode) under a
spinlock, and complete it or update the map handles only on a response
that carries its token and answers its opcode; log and drop the rest.
Give every synchronous command a unique token: the graph id and map
flags in the low 18 bits, a sequence number above. Drop the unused
cmd_lock.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
---
