From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: virtudude <virtudude@protonmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 15:02:19 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] ASoC: qcom: q6apm: tear down a graph whose PREPARE, START or
 STOP failed

When GRAPH_START times out the DSP may have started the graph in part,
but q6apm keeps reusing it: STOP sends nothing, the next prepare sends
PREPARE and START again, and the GRAPH_CLOSE result is ignored. A
speaker graph could stay silent until reboot.

Treat a failed PREPARE, START or STOP as leaving the graph in an
unknown state: mark it dead, send STOP and CLOSE, and drop it from
graph_idr once the close is acknowledged, so the next open sends a
fresh GRAPH_OPEN. Until then the graph stays wedged with its id
refused. Graphs keep a copy of their sub-graph ids so they can be
closed after the topology is gone.

The FE prepare closes and reopens a dead graph. DPCM does not prepare
a BE whose start failed again, so the LPASS BE also sets up a dead or
missing graph at start, reopening it with q6apm_graph_reopen() and
dropping a failed setup's instance with q6apm_graph_release(). Both
keep the GPR port, as freeing it
waits for the GPR RX worker, which can be waiting for the stream lock
the trigger holds.

Tested by injecting GRAPH_START, GRAPH_STOP and GRAPH_CLOSE failures on
the WSA RX graph: the stream plays again at its next start.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
